month={NA},
abstract={In order to evaluate the impacts of jellyfish bloom on pelagic ecosystems, recent studies on the distribution ecology in Tokyo Bay and the Inland Sea of Japan, and metabolic activities such as feeding, respiration were reviewed for the scyphozoan Aurelia aurita and the lobate ctenophore Bolinopsis mikado, both of them are the most dominant gelatinous plankton. Estimated feeding rates of A. aurita at an average abundance of 0.32 ind m super(-3) in Tokyo Bay during summer ranged from 0.7 to 7 mgC m super(-3) d super(-1): this feeding impact is less strong to the secondary production by the copepod Oithona davisae judging from its high production rates of 112-118 mgC m super(-3) d super(-1). But when the Aurelia population size increased and reached 5 inds m super(-3), being sometimes observed between May and August, the feeding rate of Aurelia population was higher by far than the copepod production rate. The ctenophore B. mikado in Tokyo Bay occurred at concentrations ranging from 0 to 3 inds m super(-2), from January to June, but it abruptly increased from July and reached the maximum during summer and autumn. The population clearance rate in August was calculated 240 l d super(-1). Based on these results, impacts of massive occurrence of A. aurita/B. mikado on pelagic ecosystems were discussed with respect to the competition with planktivorous fishes, nutrient regeneration via excretion by jellyfish and material cycle. Massive occurrence of jellyfish is most likely to depress the mass of planktivorous fishes as a result of competition for zooplankton, and then copepod communities decreased by jellyfish could lead to algal bloom. Some important problems to be solved before understanding jellyfish bloom on pelagic ecosystem comprehensively were also mentioned.},
