@ARTICLE{Alcaraz_1998_JoofMaSy,
type={ARTICLE},
author={Alcaraz, M and Saiz, E and Fernandez, JA and Trepat, I and Figueiras, F and Calbet, A and Bautista, B},
title={Antarctic zooplankton metabolism: Carbon requirements and ammonium excretion of salps and crustacean zooplankton in the vicinity of the Bransfield Strait during January 1994},
journal={Journal of Marine Systems},
year={1998},
volume={17},
month={NA},
abstract={Metabolic rates (oxygen consumption and ammonia excretion) of zooplankton were determined during the austral summer (January 1994), in the eastern Bransfield Strait. The study area comprised four distinct hydrographic zones: the ice edge on the western Weddell Sea, the waters of Weddell Sea origin, the southern part of the Weddell-Scotia Confluence, and the waters of Bellingshausen origin. The objectives were to estimate the fraction of primary production accounted for by the metabolism of salps and crustacean zooplankton, as well as the contribution of their excretion to the nitrogen demand of phytoplankton. Biomass-specific respiration and excretion rates of zooplankton were measured simultaneously by incubation methods at in situ temperatures. Zooplankton biomass was measured as organic C on aliquots of samples taken between 0 and 200 m. Primary production was measured by super(14)C incorporation, using photosynthesis-irradiance relationships. Primary production ranged from 179 to 1612 mg C m super(-2) day super(-1). Salps were the most abundant zooplankton group, located mainly in the northern part of the study area, where they excluded other zooplankters. Their biomass ranged from 115 to 2930 mg C m super(-2) (0-200 m). On average, their metabolic carbon requirements represented about 5% of primary production per day, and their excretion of ammonia contributed 10% of daily phytoplankton nitrogen demand. Crustacean zooplankton were of minor importance in the study area, except at the ice edge and areas of recently melted ice. Their biomass ranged from 14.5 to 494 mg C m super(-2) and they required on average 0.9% of daily primary production, while ammonia excretion accounted for around 0.4% of the phytoplankton nitrogen demand. During this cruise, salps were the most important zooplankton group from the point of view of the transfer of biogenic carbon and nitrogen recycling. However, the control exerted by zooplankton on primary producers was overall modest, and most of the phytoplankton carbon probably sedimented out of the water column.Original Abstract: L'activite metabolique (respiration et excretion d'ammonium) du mesozooplancton a ete mesuree pendant l'ete antarctique (Janvier 1994) dans la zone orientale du detroit de Bransfield. Du point de vue hydrographique, la region etudiee peut etre divisee en quatre zones: le bord de la glace dans la zone occidentale de la mer de Weddell, les eaux provenant de la mer de Weddell, la zone meridionale de la confluence Weddell-Scotia, et les eaux provenant de la Mer de Bellingshausen. Les objectifs etaient de quantifier la proportion de la production primaire necessaire pour couvrir les pertes metaboliques de carbone des salpes et des crustaces planctoniques, aussi bien que leur contribution sous forme d'excretion d'ammonium aux demandes d'azote du phytoplancton. Les taux specifiques de respiration et d'excretion d'ammonium du zooplancton ont ete mesures simultanement par incubation a temperature in situ. La biomasse du zooplancton a ete mesuree sur des aliquotes des echantillons pris entre la surface et 200 m de profondeur, et exprimee en carbone organique. La production primaire a ete mesuree par incorporation de super(14)C, et calculee d'apres des relations photosynthese-irradiance. La production primaire etait comprise entre 179 et 1612 mg C m super(-2) jour super(-1). Les salpes ont ete le groupe le plus abondant du zooplancton, surtout dans la partie nord de la zone, ou les crustaces etaient exclus. Leur biomasse etait comprise entre 115 et 2930 mg C m super(-2). En moyenne, leur demande metabolique journaliere de carbone representait environ 5% de la production primaire, et leur excretion d'ammonium representait environ 10% de la demande journaliere d'azote du phytoplancton. Les crustaces zooplanctoniques jouaient un role moins important, excepte au bord de la glace et dans les zones de fusion de glace, ou leur biomasse etait comprise entre 14.5 et 494 mg C m super(-2). Leur demande metabolique journaliere en carbone representait 0.9% de la production primaire, tandis que l'ammonium excrete contribuait a 0.4% de la demande journaliere en azote du phytoplancton. En considerant la dominance des salpes pendant la campagne et leurs pertes metaboliques, leur role dans le transfert de carbone biogenique et dans le recyclage d'azote doit etre tres important, malgre le modeste controle exerce en general par le zooplancton sur les producteurs primaires, ce qui suggere que la sedimentation est probablement le destin final de la majorite du carbone phytoplanctonique.},
